WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.288
Hi, I'm Filbert!

00:00:02.288 --> 00:00:05.613
BERT reads every token with the whole sentence in view.

00:00:05.613 --> 00:00:06.278
Here's how.

00:00:09.475 --> 00:00:12.782
Take the causal mask off, so every token sees both sides.

00:00:12.782 --> 00:00:16.088
Then hide fifteen percent of the tokens, and train the model

00:00:16.088 --> 00:00:17.291
to put them back.

00:00:19.458 --> 00:00:20.980
A decoder masks the future.

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:23.416
Each token sees only what came before it.

00:00:23.836 --> 00:00:27.399
An encoder has no next token to predict, so it keeps every

00:00:27.399 --> 00:00:29.180
pair: both sides of the context.

00:00:30.842 --> 00:00:34.672
Each token, its sentence and its position each pick a row from a table.

00:00:34.672 --> 00:00:36.039
The three rows are added.

00:00:36.459 --> 00:00:38.914
Twelve blocks of attention and MLP follow.

00:00:38.914 --> 00:00:42.069
In every block, each token reads the whole sequence.

00:00:42.489 --> 00:00:44.381
Out comes one vector per token.

00:00:44.381 --> 00:00:47.850
At the hidden slots, a small head guesses the original word.

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:51.472
For a task, take the vector of the first token, called CLS,

00:00:51.472 --> 00:00:53.340
and train one new layer on it.

00:00:54.775 --> 00:00:57.397
Pick fifteen percent of the positions at random.

00:00:57.817 --> 00:01:00.075
Eighty percent of those become a mask token.

00:01:00.526 --> 00:01:02.586
Ten percent become a random token.

00:01:03.679 --> 00:01:05.314
And ten percent stay as they are.

00:01:06.388 --> 00:01:09.335
It must predict the original at all of them, so it can't

00:01:09.335 --> 00:01:10.563
trust any token it sees.

00:01:11.983 --> 00:01:15.178
BERT also guessed whether sentence B really followed sentence A.

00:01:15.178 --> 00:01:17.734
Half the time it was a random one.

00:01:18.154 --> 00:01:22.681
A careful retrain a year later dropped that task, and matched or beat BERT.

00:01:22.681 --> 00:01:25.591
Fresh masks, more data and bigger batches mattered more.

00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:28.531
Encoders kept improving.

00:01:28.531 --> 00:01:32.724
ModernBERT reads over eight thousand tokens, with rotary positions.

00:01:35.058 --> 00:01:38.129
Seeing both sides makes an encoder a strong, cheap reader.

00:01:38.129 --> 00:01:40.278
It's also why it can't write efficiently.

00:01:42.983 --> 00:01:43.758
No mask.

00:01:43.758 --> 00:01:44.533
Hidden tokens.

00:01:44.533 --> 00:01:45.696
One vector out.

00:01:47.583 --> 00:01:49.747
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:49.747 --> 00:01:50.365
I'm Filbert.

00:01:50.365 --> 00:01:50.674
Bye!
